{{also|Grenade|grénade}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{borrowing|fr|grenade|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|grenate|lang=fro}} in the phrase {{term|pomme grenate||pomegranate|lang=fro}}, ultimately from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|pomum||apple|lang=la}} + {{term|granatum||having grains|lang=la}}. The ''-d'' developed in French under influence of Spanish {{term|granada|lang=es}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɡɹəˈneɪd/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪd|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A [[pomegranate]].
# A small [[explosive]] [[device]], designed to be thrown by hand or launched from a grenade launcher.
# {{context|heraldiccharge|lang=en}} A [[charge]] similar to a [[fireball]], and made of a disc-shaped bomb shell, but with only one set of flames at the top.

